course: dictionary
lesson: 3499
title: A job word in context
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words name a job, and the words around them tell you what the job involves. Read the whole sentence and ask what the people or animals in it need. «When a farrier nails a metal shoe onto a horse's foot, the horse does not mind.» The clues are horse, foot and metal shoe, so a farrier must work on horses' feet.
explain: Now check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence: «When a person who looks after horses' hooves and fits horseshoes nails a metal shoe onto a horse's foot, the horse does not mind.» It still makes sense, so the meaning fits.
explain: Sometimes the text explains the reason too. «A horse's hoof wall is like a thick fingernail with no feeling, so a farrier can nail on a shoe without hurting it.» The word so links cause and effect: the hoof has no feeling, so the nailing does not hurt.
fact: A farrier is a person whose job is to look after horses' hooves and fit horseshoes on them.
fact: In the past, a farrier could also treat sick horses, a little like an animal doctor.
fact: Farriers visit stables every few weeks to trim hooves and fit new shoes.
fact: A hoof is made of keratin, the same material as your fingernails.
fact: The outer hoof wall has no feeling, so a properly fitted shoe does not hurt the horse.
exercise: «When a farrier nails a metal shoe onto a horse's foot, the horse does not mind.» Who is a farrier? | A builder who makes stables | A rider who trains horses for races | A horse that has lost a shoe | *A person who looks after horses' hooves and fits horseshoes
exercise: Sam says a farrier must be a doctor for people. What does the text say a farrier could do in the past? | Teach children to ride | Make cakes for the stable | Sell hay by the bale | *Treat sick horses, a little like an animal doctor
exercise: «A horse's hoof wall is like a thick fingernail with no feeling, so a farrier can nail on a shoe without hurting it.» Why does the nailing not hurt the horse? | Because the farrier uses a soft hammer | *Because the outer hoof wall has no feeling | Because the shoe is made of wood | Because horses like loud noises
test: In old stories, where does a farrier work? | *In a forge with fire, an anvil and a hammer | On a ship at sea | In a school hall | A library full of books
test: «Hooves keep growing and need trimming every few weeks.» How often do hooves need trimming? | Only once in a horse's life | Every few years | *Every few weeks | Every day at midnight
exam: Ella reads «a farrier nails a shoe on» and does not know farrier. What is the best way to check her meaning? | Guess from the title of the book alone | Look only at the last word of the sentence | *Swap in a meaning, such as someone who fits horseshoes, and see if the sentence still makes sense | Ignore the word and never read on
